      <workList xml:id="workDesc_N20344">
         <work xml:id="work_id4afcb77a">
            <identifier label="SchW" xml:id="identifier_N20348">C1:001</identifier>
            <title xml:id="title_id8524e2f8" xml:lang="de">Abhandlung vom Ursprunge und Alter der Musik</title>
            <contributor xml:id="respStmt_N20356">
               <persName role="dedicatee" xml:id="persName_03364385">Société royal des sciences</persName>
            </contributor>
            <creation xml:id="creation_id41aad4ee">
               <date isodate="1754" xml:id="date_df62b40a">1754</date>
            </creation>
            <history xml:id="history_idf5746284">
               <p xml:id="idm86">In November 1752, while Scheibe was living in Sønderborg, <persName xml:id="idm87" auth.uri="http://www.viaf.org" auth="VIAF" role="">King Frederik V</persName> presented the following question to some scholars in Copenhagen: ‘Who was the first singer from whom vocal music originated, and among which people did it first spread?’ (pp. xii–xiii) The king remembered Scheibe, and through <persName xml:id="idm88" auth.uri="http://www.viaf.org" auth="VIAF" role="">Count Frederik Raben</persName> he asked him to formulate some thoughts on the issue and send them to the The Royal Society of Sciences. After four or five days, having consulted his own books as well as those of 'a learned friend', Scheibe sent his response to Raben. The count then handed over the manuscript to <persName xml:id="idm89" auth.uri="http://www.viaf.org" auth="VIAF" role="">Johan Ludvig Holstein</persName>, the president of the academy, who presented it to Frederik V. The king liked the dissertation and ordered the academy to publish it. Since it had been written in a very short time and under pressure, Scheibe wished to revise the work and received permission to do so. He apparently finished the essay in Sønderborg in August 1753 when he signed the dedication. Scheibe chose to use his publishers in Flensburg, most likely because they were closer to Sønderborg thus making the proof-reading phase easier. The book was dedicated to The Royal Society of Sciences in Copenhagen and the dedication copy, stamped ‘S* R* S*’ on the binding, is shelved in The Royal Library today. Besides the history of the publication, the foreword also explains how Scheibe approached the subject. He starts with the Bible, ancient history and mythology, then proceeds throughout history referring to <persName xml:id="idm90" auth.uri="http://www.viaf.org" auth="VIAF" role="">Marcus Meibom</persName> as well as <persName xml:id="idm91" auth.uri="http://www.viaf.org" auth="VIAF" role="">Johann Mattheson</persName>. He also deals with various definitions of musical terms and names, refers to several of his other publications, and includes a detailed section on the establishment of The Musical Society in 1750. He also mentions some of his works that were performed at the society (SchW B2:300, B2:429, B2:430 B2:436, B2:439, and B7:001). The same year as it was issued, 1754, the book received a lengthy review by the composer <persName xml:id="idm92" auth.uri="http://www.viaf.org" auth="VIAF" role="">Friedrich Wilhelm Riedt</persName> in <rend xml:id="idm93" fontstyle="italic">Historisch-kritische Beyträge</rend>, and four years later another one appeared in <rend xml:id="idm94" fontstyle="italic">Fortgesetzte Nachrichten</rend>.</p>
            </history>
